AB  - Interpreting perspective-dependent spatial referring expressions is a key challenge for intuitive human-robot interaction, as users naturally communicate in egocentric terms. We present an interactive spatial grounding system that integrates mixed reality (MR) sensing with LLM-based reasoning to translate free-form spatial language into actionable robot targets. Using a unified table-centric representation, the backend computes viewpoint- and robot-relative spatial features and ranks candidate targets under underspecified constraints. We further apply confirmation-gated execution, which visualizes the current target hypothesis in MR and triggers robot motion only after explicit user confirmation. Overall, this demo focuses on enabling robots to understand user-centric, perspective-dependent spatial references by grounding them in MR for tabletop manipulation. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
