AB  - This paper presents a technological solution designed to support social interaction and inclusion for individuals with Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) during tabletop gameplay with neurotypical and autistic peers. The work explores the integration of large language models (LLMs), computer vision (CV), and Extended Reality (XR) to create an assistive system that reduces barriers to participation in social gaming contexts. A prototype is developed that augments Dixit, a narrativebased card game, with a voice-activated virtual assistant that provides contextual guidance, rule clarification, and decision support throughout gameplay. The system employs OpenAI's realtime model for conversational interaction, YOLOv8n-based CV for detecting physical game components, and GPT-4o for card interpretation. The implementation demonstrates the technical feasibility of this multi-modal approach and establishes a foundation for future user-centered evaluation and stakeholder engagement to assess its effectiveness in promoting autonomy and social participation for individuals with ASD. © 2026 IEEE.
