AB  - This paper presents a bibliometric mapping of generative AI in game development from 2017 to 2025, covering 1,103 publications retrieved under a dual-axis query of model families (e.g., GANs, diffusion models, LLMs) and development components (e.g., PCG, game assets, NPCs, engines). Using CiteSpace and VOSviewer, we analyze co-occurrence, co-citation, and cluster structures to reveal the field's evolution from algorithm-driven (genetic/evolutionary) to data-driven (deep learning, GAN) and experience-driven (LLM-NPCs, dynamic difficulty, serious games) trajectories. To meet EI/Scopus expectations for computer-application rigor and reproducibility, we document the end-to-end computing workflow (query to de-duplication to text normalization to co-occurrence/cluster construction), fix random seeds, and report lightweight engineering metrics (step-wise runtime and peak memory). We also list software versions and key parameters (time slicing, Pathfinder pruning, LLR labeling; modularity Q and silhouette S) to make figures and tables auditable. The study consolidates trends, pinpoints core venues and authors, and offers a concise, reusable recipe for bibliometric mining in AI-for-games
