TI  - Feasibility of LLM-Augmented VR Micro-Coaching for Children with ADHD
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 the 11th International Conference on Computer and Communication Systems, ICCCS 2026
SP  - 1172
EP  - 1176
DO  - 10.1109/ICCCS69761.2026.11613247
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047033570?origin=resultslist
AB  - Attention-training interventions for children with Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) often struggle to deliver personalized, in-the-moment guidance. Virtual Reality (VR) offers ecological engagement but lacks flexible, languagedriven micro-coaching and clinician-facing provenance. We developed and evaluated an Large language models (LLMs)-augmented VR system that delivers brief, grounded microcoaching during gameplay and generates concise, provenancebacked session summaries for clinicians and caregivers. In a proof-of-concept pilot, participants completed two immersive scenarios while the runtime fused task events and low-cost EEG telemetry, a constrained LLM reasoning layer that emitted short coaching prompts and bounded actions, a contextual-bandit personalization policy, and a clinician dashboard for logging and review. Usability, task performance, safety, and implementation tradeoffs were recorded. All participants completed exploration and task phases without adverse events; mean in-session task accuracy was 92.8%. Implementation notes include real-time runtime integration, latency and hallucination mitigation strategies, and clinician-editable summaries. Findings demonstrate functional feasibility of LLM micro-coaching in a live VR runtime but do not establish clinical efficacy. This work introduces a practical architecture and early evidence that constrained LLMs can provide useful, real-time micro-coaching in VR. © 2026 IEEE.
