AB  - This study aims to precisely classify user demands using generative artificial intelligence tools, addressing the issue of insufficient quantification of users' emotional needs in cultural and creative product design related to Song Brocade patterns. It proposes an innovative design strategy based on the Theory of Qualia and generative AI technology to enhance the cultural identity and market competitiveness of such products. A qualia model is constructed around product imagery, behavioral preference, and emotional identity as its three core dimensions. The Kano model and K-means clustering analysis are employed to classify the preferences of 129 users. The Song Brocade patterns are generated and trained using the LoRA model to achieve visual optimization, with the design outcomes subsequently validated through user evaluation. According to the K-means clustering analysis of user classifications, the “Cultured-Elegant” group prefers historical connotations, while the “Functional-Practical” group focuses on convenience. Based on these findings, culturally adaptive patterns are generated. This strategy provides significant insights for the refined development and industrial advancement of intangible cultural heritage cultural and creative products.
