AB  - Pla-tsugi: Terrain as Fracture reimagines fracture not as loss but as a generative condition. A stone collected from the Rokko Mountain Range (Kobe, Japan) is regarded not as an isolated object but as a fragment once belonging to a continuous terrain. Through 3D scanning, the stone’s geometry is digitally aligned with topographic data from Japan’s Geospatial Information Authority [Geospatial Information Authority of Japan (GSI), 2024], repositioning the fragment within a larger geological context. Mixed Reality (MR) tools—Meta Quest 3 and Gravity Sketch—were employed to design connective forms conceived as poetic sutures rather than structural joints, emphasizing gestures of care over closure. Materialized through PLA-based 3D printing, these sutures merge with the stone, producing hybrid works where natural matter and digital reconstruction intersect. In exhibitions, viewers are invited to cradle palm-sized fragments, transforming spectatorship into intimate participation. For international presentations, where transporting the original stone is not possible, high-resolution replicas are used to preserve tactile immediacy.This work belongs to the “Pla-tsugi” series, a neologism combining “plastic” and kintsugi (the Japanese art of ceramic repair with gold) [Harada, 2024]. It extends the Japanese repair philosophy from vessels to environments, proposing repair as an act of reconnection between humans, materials, and landscapes. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
