TI  - Self-Reference and Medium-Reference in Virtual Reality and Trompe l’Oeil
PY  - 2024
T2  - Art and Artifice in Visual Culture: Eighteenth Century to the Present
SP  - 172
EP  - 187
DO  - 10.4324/9781003478898-13
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85215210336?origin=resultslist
AB  - This chapter explores the strikingly similar ontological and epistemological conundrums of virtual reality (VR), augmented reality (AR), generative artificial intelligence (AI), and the traditions of trompe l’oeil. VR, AR, and immersive digital environments of various kinds sit at the cutting edge of individual, societal, and regulatory rapports with reality, understood as either (or both) physical reality and reality-as-truth. However, they are yet to be discussed in relation to the centuries-old, multicultural exploration of artifice, particularly in its extreme manifestation in styles and genres such as trompe l’oeil and hyperrealism. Engaging with the philosophical and art-historical literature on artifice and the interdisciplinary literature on immersive digital worlds, this chapter investigates if and how VR/AR artifacts are self- or meta-referential. In particular, the chapter articulates the mechanisms of art-making and the visual language that gives form to the self-conscious, meta-referential aspects of both historical and contemporary representations. Sensitive to the diversity of periods, cultures, and mediums that shape examples of both trompe l’oeil and VR/AR, the chapter contextualizes points of convergence and divergence within a variegated tapestry of expressions of artifice. © 2025 selection and editorial matter, Sonia Coman, Vasile-Ovidiu Prejmerean, and Michael Yonan; individual chapters, the contributors.
