TI  - Self extending reality (S-XR): from facial recognition to “tachymorphic media” amidst the emergence of resting AI face
PY  - 2026
T2  - Frontiers in Virtual Reality
VL  - 7
DO  - 10.3389/frvir.2026.1756920
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042227493?origin=resultslist
AB  - This paper reframes extended reality (XR) as media, specifically focusing on self-extending reality (S-XR) media, where the face is the interface for coupling computing and environment data in real time. Centering the face as interface in that ontology recasts facial recognition technologies beyond “face-data-retrieval” logics toward “face-data-interaction” logics. The paper then focuses on two theoretical contributions for these XR media. First it argues that dominant, display-science taxonomies stabilize an electro–atomic divide and understate how XR reconfigures reality through radical relations between electronic and atomic domains. The paper defines XR as real-time relations between computing-data and environment-data to clarify the stakes when such relations become intimate. The second contribution is conceptual and programmatic: S-XR names systems that mediate identity at machine speed through biospatial capture via the face-as-interface. To move beyond exclusively clinical framings of external identity manipulation here, the paper introduces “tachymorphic media” to evaluate and design S-XR that can include experimentation, play, and care, while still acknowledging platform ambivalence and cultural paradox. It concludes with introducing normative governance and design principles for S-XR including active consent, generative diversity, building techno-economic literacies, and valuing joy, pleasure, and curiosity. These all privilege plural, user-authored identities over homogenizing defaults. In other words, there is an imperative for designing user-friendly (Illich, Deschooling society, 1971) facial interfaces. If S-XR clarifies how identity is operated at machine speed, tachymorphic media offer a vocabulary for evaluating and building systems that keep identity formable amidst emerging conditions that normalize “resting AI face”. Copyright © 2026 Heemsbergen.
