TI  - Safety Assessment of 3D Generation Models in AR/VR Applications
PY  - 2025
T2  - LAMPS 2025 - Proceedings of the 2nd ACM Workshop on Large AI Systems and Models with Privacy and Security Analysis
SP  - 30
EP  - 38
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3733800.3763268
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105024916098?origin=resultslist
AB  - The rapid advancements in 3D generative models are revolutionizing content creation for immersive applications like Virtual Reality (VR) and Augmented Reality (AR), offering unprecedented capabilities for complex and realistic 3D asset generation. However, this transformative power introduces significant safety concerns, particularly regarding the unintentional or malicious generation of unsafe content. Unlike text or 2D image models, 3D content poses unique risks due to its immersive, interactive nature, with potential implications across digital environments and real-world scenarios such as 3D printing. To confront this challenge, we carry out the first systematic safety audit of text-to-3D generation. Using 6 137 prompts across five safety suites, we sample a state-of-the-art text-to-3D workflow and double-blind-annotate every output. Alarmingly, 829 objects (13.51%) are flagged as unsafe, confirming persistent risk in current generation workflows. We then propose a novel multimodal safety classifier that fuses geometric and visual information. We use a MeshCNN encoder to distill shape features from simplified meshes, while a visual encoder captures complementary appearance information from multi-view renderings. The resulting embeddings are fused and fed to a residual multilayer perceptron tuned for safety prediction. Comprehensive experiments demonstrate the superior efficacy of our method. Our combined multimodal classifier achieves an accuracy of 0.8784 and an AUC-ROC of 0.8775, surpassing leading image safety classification baselines, such as Unsafe Diffusion, ShieldGemma2, and LlamaGuard4. Additionally, ablation studies confirm that multimodal fusion consistently outperforms either modality in isolation, underscoring the value of joint geometry-image reasoning for 3D safety. By bridging the existing research gap in 3D generative model safety, this work provides actionable insights and a robust framework for the responsible deployment of AI-driven 3D content creation technologies, ensuring safer and more trustworthy immersive experiences. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/xitang2000/3D_safety_assessment. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
