VL  - 40
IS  - 1
DO  - 10.1061/JCCEE5.CPENG-6727
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105019037539?origin=resultslist
AB  - Geometric quality assessment (QA) in construction requires precise dimensional validation to ensure structural safety and compliance. While mixed reality (MR) technology promises enhanced inspection capabilities, current methods face fundamental limitations in spatial registration accuracy, environmental adaptability, and semantic understanding. This research advances the MR-based QA through three key contributions: 1) a mathematical framework for enhanced spatial registration via the proposed fitting plane enhanced raycasting technique, which transcends hardware constraints to enable accurate measurement of large-scale elements; 2) a technical solution for dual-modality imaging that ensures measurement robustness across diverse environmental conditions; and 3) a generalizable semantic understanding approach that eliminates traditional vocabulary constraints in building element recognition. Field experiments demonstrate robust performance of the proposed framework: 92% median IoU in element segmentation; measurement errors of 2% for regular shapes; and 8% for irregular shapes, while maintaining a 4% error rate in low-light conditions. These advancements transform construction inspection practices by enabling reliable round-the-clock assessment capability and automated dimensional analysis across diverse building elements. This work advances MR-based QA and establishes a foundation for next-generation construction inspection methods that blend precision with efficiency. © 2025 American Society of Civil Engineers.
