TI  - EMBRACING THE CHALLENGES OF VISUALISING UNFAMILIAR FAMILIAR SPACES
PY  - 2022
T2  - Modelling and Simulation 2022 - European Simulation and Modelling Conference, ESM 2022
SP  - 70
EP  - 75
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85160823295?origin=resultslist
AB  - In our post-digital era, fundamental definitions of architecture are undergoing radical transformations as a result of advanced technological disruption to traditional architectural theory and practice. Digital networks, information technology, digital modelling, and simulation systems have widened the scope of otherwise quite conventional views of the architecture realm, leading to an increasing awareness of the scope of virtual spatial design. Virtual environments, unrestricted by laws of physics, provide dynamic encounters that can add new considerations to architectural interpretation and therefore a more compelling experience. Virtual exhibition of architecture facilitates understanding concepts and artefacts at various and flexible scales and proportions. Teleportation and navigation through unreal and surreal spatial experiences enable visitors to explore architectural space from completely new viewpoints, in many cases impossible in the physical realm. In terms of architectural knowledge, understanding geometry, materiality, three-dimensional shape, scale, movement, and a sense of space are crucial to appreciating concepts behind an artefact. Often it can be challenging to articulate the intricate processes that take place in the backdrop of architectural creation. Attempting to unveil the mystery of an architectural masterpiece by altering the fluidity of the simulated environment is a worthwhile pursuit of higher knowledge, which would otherwise be more difficult to elucidate using analogue media. This paper reports on our investigations into the possibility of understanding architectural space and form in a more-than-real context through curating a virtual exhibition of aspects of Catalan architect Antoni Gaudí’s formal design process for the Sagrada Família Basilica in Barcelona, Spain. Our paper eludes specifically to the Sala Creuer (Crossing Hall), the nearly completed auditorium space situated 70+ metres above the crossing at the centre of the basilica, and forming the base of the central tower. The immersive virtual reality system reported here enables visitors to traverse an unfamiliar familiar environment while inhabiting virtual avatars. The environment is familiar in the sense that it is an occupiable space in terms of architectural elements such as floors, walls, ceilings, windows, etc., but otherwise unfamiliar inasmuch as the enclosed space is a highly singular interior comprising very few planar surfaces: horizontal floor surfaces meld with vertical as the vertical do with very exaggerated ceiling elements. Some of the access elements are transparent and minimally suspended in space. Virtual visitors can engage and interact with this unfamiliar familiar environment, with the underlying architectural intentions and meaning gradually revealed. During the visitors’ exploration of the virtual Sala Creuer, generative animations seek to aid in explaining how Gaudí’s complex geometries merge and evolve to generate yet more novel forms. We have exploited the dynamic characters of the virtual medium to create engaging spatial experiences for the visitor, and to help them to derive a unique and augmented appreciation for architecture. © ESM 2022. All rights reserved.
