TI  - Evaluation of Generative AI for Synthesizing Audio on Non-human Creature 3D Content
PY  - 2026
T2  - Communications in Computer and Information Science
VL  - 3052 CCIS
SP  - 51
EP  - 58
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-30836-8_7
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044968103?origin=resultslist
AB  - 3D contents such as games or virtual reality are typically composed of multiple assets that are also composed of multimodal information such as 3D models and audio. With recent advances in AI, there is growing interest in utilizing generative AI to automatically create both the 3D model and audio assets. In the audio field, although prior work has explored AI-generated human speech and general audio such as ambient soundscapes like traffic or forest noise, there is only limited exploration on audio-voice from non-human creatures such as animals. This study aims to evaluate several state-of-the-art generative AI models’ performances to generate a clean audio for a single non-human creature 3D model for each of their actions. I prepared a set of test datasets consisting of videos generated from 3D content development software. The videos are generated through animating non-human creature 3D models and have no original ground truth of audio data. In several cases, the generative AI can generate audio that matches the motion of the 3D model in the video. However, I also noted that sometimes the generative AI models fail to generate clean audio, where microphone noise and background ambient soundscapes are still included in the generated audio. These findings highlight both the potential and current limitations of the latest generative AI in generating audio assets for non-human creatures. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
