TI  - Zero-Shot and Foundation Model Performance on Face Emotion Recognition for Avatars in XR - Challenges, Limitations, and Potentials
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings - 2026 IEEE Conference on Virtual Reality and 3D User Interfaces Abstracts and Workshops, VRW 2026
SP  - 482
EP  - 488
DO  - 10.1109/VRW70859.2026.00092
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105038796105?origin=resultslist
AB  - Avatars are increasingly used as primary user representations in XR systems, making accurate interpretation of their emotional expressions essential for inclusive and trustworthy human-computer interaction. However, facial emotion recognition (FER) models are predominantly trained on real-faces, and their behavior on avatarlike representations remains poorly understood due to the lack of annotated avatar emotion datasets. In this work, we systematically evaluate two approaches: a pre-trained FER model (HSEmotion) trained on real-face data, and a large-scale foundation model (CLIP) without task-specific fine-tuning. We assess their performance on both real-face (FERV-39k) and avatar-like (FaceExpressions-70k) datasets. Using labeled real-face data and expression-emotion mappings from the literature, we analyze model behavior, performance differences, and sources of uncertainty in avatar-based settings. Our results show that emotion-specific models exhibit stronger alignment with established expression-emotion relationships, while foundation models face limitations in fine-grained emotion inference. This work supports more transparent and inclusive deployment of affective technologies in XR-systems. © 2026 IEEE.
