TI  - Chinese Landscape Painting Composition Generation and Spatial Layout Optimization Using Transformer-Diffusion Hybrid Model
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the 2nd International Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Virtual Reality and Interaction Design
SP  - 729
EP  - 735
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3820915.3821027
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3820915.3821027
AB  - Recent advancements in deep generative models have shown remarkable success in image synthesis; however, generating traditional Chinese landscape paintings remains a significant challenge. Existing models, such as Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) and standard Diffusion Models, inherently rely on Western single-point perspective priors and struggle to capture unique Eastern aesthetic principles. Consequently, they often fail to replicate the “scatter perspective” (multi-point perspective) and “Ji Bai Dang Hei” (intentional blank space), resulting in flattened spatial hierarchies and noise-polluted blank areas. To address these limitations, this paper proposes a novel Transformer-Diffusion hybrid architecture specifically tailored for Chinese landscape painting synthesis. First, we introduce a Three-Distances Spatial Encoder (TSE) to mathematically formulate the scatter perspective, enabling the Transformer to act as a global planner that constructs rational spatial hierarchies across high, deep, and level distances. Second, a Virtual-Real Attention Mechanism (VRAM) is proposed to dynamically modulate the U-Net denoising process, actively preserving the purity of intentional blank spaces. We construct a specialized dataset comprising 320 topologically-annotated classical masterpieces and 180 structural sketches. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our hybrid model significantly outperforms state-of-the-art baselines. Quantitatively, our method achieves a Frechet Inception Distance (FID) of 21.4 and establishes new benchmarks on domain-specific metrics, improving the Spatial Hierarchy Score (SHS) by 29.3% and the Blankness Rationality Ratio (BRR) by 18.5%. This research provides a mathematically rigorous paradigm for integrating classical art theories into neural network design, advancing the digitization of cultural heritage.
