AB  - As a key medium for coordinating natural ecosystems and artificial built environments, landscape architecture design integrates ecological functions, aesthetic values, and social cultural attributes. Traditional design paradigms often rely on subjective experience and judgment, which are limited by insufficient data support and weak quantitative analysis. With the in-depth application of big data analysis and intelligent algorithms, landscape architecture design is gradually transforming towards a data-driven and model-optimized scientific direction. By mining and machine learning from massive environmental data on meteorology, soil, vegetation coverage, human behavior trajectories, heat maps of pedestrian flow, activity preferences, and cultural geographic information, designers can construct multi-dimensional decision-making models to achieve precise identification of site characteristics, ecological sensitivity assessment, and prediction of usage demands. Moreover, the integration of intelligent generative design, parametric simulation, and virtual reality (VR) technologies enables dynamic verification of the spatial efficiency and ecological adaptability of design schemes, thereby optimizing core indicators such as landscape patterns, microclimate regulation, and resource circulation. This paper systematically demonstrates the methodological value of big data and intelligent technologies in landscape architecture design and proposes a design strategy framework based on data modeling, intelligent feedback, and collaborative platforms, providing theoretical basis and practical paths for technological upgrading in the industry.
