DO  - 10.1109/GCCE.2018.8574828
AB  - This paper explains the modeling and verification of the operational behavior of a virtual reality (VR) tourism system for the architectural design during the system requirement definition phase of the system development life cycle. Accurately modeled operational behavior can provide deep insights into the navigational transitions through events and activities and helps us identify and quantify the resources being used in the system. This research also aims to identify the most common and initial behavior of tourists, such as participation in events such as photography, paragliding, and hunting events. Also, we can evaluate the complex operational properties of the process of navigation, such as the possibility of accidents during participation in events. This will support the design and development of better and more realistic systems under actual operating conditions. By using the Petri net design tool Hierarchical Petri Net Simulator, we realized an accurate model and analyzed navigation behavior. Further, we generated an operational profile-based testing framework to improve the reliability of the VR application.
