SP  - 720
EP  - 724
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3777730.3777845
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3777730.3777845
AB  - To address the persistent misalignment between individual aesthetic preferences and semantic intent in interactive art creation, this paper proposes a preference-feedback–driven optimization paradigm designed to balance generation quality, diversity, and stability under constrained latency. Methodologically, we construct a closed-loop framework—“preference acquisition → reward composition → proximal policy optimization → adaptive scheduling → controllable generation.” The framework fuses multi-source indicators with user preferences, employs PPO for stable policy updates, and leverages adaptive weighting with KL scheduling to curb distributional drift and reconcile multiple objectives. Experiments conducted on a unified backbone and evaluation protocol compare the proposed approach against an unaligned baseline, an aesthetics-guided post-processing method, and PPO without adaptivity. Results show consistent gains in semantic alignment and aesthetic scores (e.g., CLIP from 0.318 to 0.334; Aesthetic from 6.58 to 6.92), a substantial reduction in FID (29.7 to 26.8), and near-constant end-to-end latency; pairwise user studies also exhibit statistically significant win rates over all baselines. The contributions are threefold: (1) a reusable PPO-based adaptive alignment framework that integrates preference learning with generative control; (2) a stable training and evaluation protocol tailored to interactive settings; and (3) joint optimization of semantic alignment, aesthetic quality, diversity, and stability without incurring notable latency, offering a methodological foundation for practical deployment of personalized art generation systems.
