AB  - Multiple primary lung cancers (MPLC) are positioned within a perioperative blind spot of identified but not clinically visible patient risk correlatives. We have addressed this problem through development of a dual-modal connected healthcare system that leverages smartphone cough acoustic sensing in conjunction with consumer-grade virtual-reality (VR) surgical-twin technology for patient-level perioperative risk prediction. All evaluations were conducted retrospectively using all previous archival publicly available data sets and were analyzed for the presence of cough sound data (Coswara, COUGHVID, UK COVID-19 Vocal Audio), imaging data (LIDC-IDRI, NSCLC-Radiomics, Lung-PET-CT-Dx) and newly identified surgical workflow data (Cholec80, HeiChole, AutoLaparo), with the pooled dataset collection spanning three continents only in aggregate across modalities. Using four established independent perioperative-pulmonary-complication anchor points, the final performance of our fusion mechanism demonstrated an area under the curve (AUC) 0.88 (0.85–0.90). Because no thoracic surgical video exists at foundation-model scale, the VR-twin branch is trained on abdominal-workflow proxies for phasere-cognition features only; the system therefore contributes a faithfulness-supervised cross-modal fusion architecture validated on cross-domain proxies, rather than a fully thoracic-native teleoncology deployment. © 1975-2011 IEEE.
