TI  - Computational Analysis of Emotional Coding Characteristics in Female Artists' Works from the Perspective of Neural Plasticity
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the 2nd International Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Virtual Reality and Interaction Design
SP  - 1133
EP  - 1138
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3820915.3821092
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3820915.3821092
AB  - In the era of increasingly precise intelligent generation of artistic works, model algorithms excel in simulating the style of artists' works but overlook the original pursuit of art, which is to convey emotions. How can the emotional state of an artist be accurately conveyed to the viewer. In our research, we establish a system for AI learning through emotional encoding, mapping the emotional characteristics of artists during creation to quantifiable visual elements such as color, line, composition, and texture of the works. If these characteristics can be decoded by the audience and evoke the corresponding emotional response that the artist intended to convey, it means that a path to enhancing AI emotional intelligence may be found. Firstly, by selecting a recent exhibition to collect a dataset of artistic works, we develop an emotional evaluation method called Emotional Encoding Score (EES) based on three dimensions: emotional granularity, evocative potential, and the balance between concept and emotion. Subsequently, to verify the hypothesis that female artists' works express richer emotions, we draw on the principles of neuroaesthetics and neuroplasticity to further analyze representative works of female artists in postmodernism. We aim to identify the factors and strengths that influence emotional encoding, improve the existing model's analysis of emotions, and establish a closer dialogue between generative art, traditional painting, and aesthetic education.
