TI  - A Lightweight Image-Recognition WebAR Narrative System for Low-Barrier Digital Prototyping in Wellness-Oriented Cultural Tourism Parks
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the 2026 International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Generative Design
SP  - 182
EP  - 187
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3830911.3830939
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3830911.3830939
AB  - Wellness-oriented cultural tourism parks often contain local cultural narratives, health-oriented product knowledge, educational materials, and situated interpretive resources, yet they frequently face practical constraints when introducing digital interpretation systems. Native applications, heavy AR infrastructures, and high-fidelity 3D assets may increase development costs, maintenance burdens, and entry barriers for visitors. To address these constraints, this paper presents a lightweight image-recognition WebAR narrative system for low-barrier digital prototyping in wellness-oriented cultural tourism parks. The system runs in a mobile browser, supports QR/URL-based access, recognizes physical image targets, and triggers multimodal interpretive content, including images, audio, video, role prompts, mini-tasks, progress feedback, and AR-like HUD overlays. Using the Bao Gu-and-Chinese Mugwort cultural-historical anecdote as the narrative basis of the Chinese Mugwort Healer Quest prototype, the system integrates MindAR image tracking, A-Frame/Three.js rendering, target-attached HUD presentation, local progress gating, and explicit media-triggering mechanisms. Its runtime flow follows a sequence of detecting a physical marker, checking the expected station, attaching a reusable HUD to the detected target, rendering the current narrative phase, and unlocking the next station after user interaction. Rather than reporting comparative user-experience results, this paper focuses on system architecture, key implementation logic, and a field technical validation checklist. The contribution is a deployable and maintainable WebAR prototyping approach for low-cost digital interpretation in resource-constrained wellness-oriented cultural tourism environments.
