AB  - Extended Reality (XR) is transforming the way users engage in learning and training through immersive, interactive environments. This survey analyzes adaptive XR learning systems, focusing on AI-driven personalization and multimodal interaction. We examine how systems use diverse natural signals such as gaze, speech, gestures to model user states and dynamically adapt feedback in real-time. Unlike prior reviews that treat XR-based systems, multimodal integration and adaptive learning independently, this work provides a detailed synthesis of their convergence by reviewing recent developments in XR learning environments, highlighting the use of large language models (LLMs) for context-aware adaptation and guidance generation. Key platforms, including SMARTe-VR, XaiR and SIGMA, are examined alongside multimodal benchmarks like Ego4D, HOT3D, and HoloAssist. By identifying architectural trends and adaptation strategies, we highlight ongoing challenges such as data fusion, user modeling and data privacy concerns. This survey outlines future directions toward scalable, highly personalized XR learning systems powered by AI and rich sensor data. © 2025 IEEE.
