SP  - 79
EP  - 87
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3772128.3772143
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3772128.3772143
AB  - This paper presents an algorithmic framework for the three-dimensional (3D) reconstruction of facial features based on the 3D Morphable Model (3DMM), utilizing controllable 3D model deformations derived from 3DMM facial feature vectors. By using a single-view 2D facial image as input, the method enables rapid and effective 3D reconstruction of the face model. An improved generative framework for 3D reconstruction of facial images based on 3DMM is proposed. In response to the facial feature recognition deficiencies in AI-generated facial images within the DECA framework, improvements are made to the backbone network for the recognition of 68 key facial landmarks, enabling the spatial 3D estimation of 2D feature points. Furthermore, to address the issue of feature mapping discrepancies in the generated virtual faces, an average human face is used for alignment with the 3D reference model. To reduce the reliance on large-scale real facial data and overcome challenges such as the inconsistent feature labeling formats in reference networks, this paper constructs a virtual human application model for 3D reconstruction of facial expressions. This offers an effective solution for the virtual human technology domain. Experimental results show that the proposed method is highly applicable to generative 3D face reconstruction across various facial expressions, especially in the domain of virtual human interaction simulation, making it significant both theoretically and in terms of practical innovation.
